AUDIO: '[music] &gt;&gt; Hi. Welcome to another video. If you were to ask me to summarize the June month for AI, then I wouldn\'t say that it was a month of big models that are crazy, but instead I would say that the month was actually the month of'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'local and small models that you can run locally. This isn\'t to say that the bigger models were bad, but the performance of the new local models have been pushed a lot. You have models like Gemma, Ornith, and everything, which is crazy to see.'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But there\'s another model now called Agents A1. It is a 35B mixture of experts agentic model built by Intern Science, which is actually the company which has made models like the Intern LM in the past.'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Intern Science is basically the team out of the Shanghai AI Laboratory, and they\'ve released this one under the Apache 2.0 license, which means you can use it for whatever you want, even commercially. So, that\'s great. The model is actually'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'based on Qwen 3.5 35B A3B. The A3B part means that even though the total parameter count is 35 billion, only about 3 billion parameters are active at any given time. This is why it runs fast locally. You get the knowledge'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of a 35B model with the speed of a 3B model, which is just amazing for local use. Their whole tagline is scaling the horizon, not the parameters. Instead of making the model bigger, they trained it on really long agentic trajectories,'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about 45,000 tokens each on average with full action, observation, and verification traces. So, the model has literally been trained on how to work like an agent, not just how to chat. This one seems like a really good local'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model. It has a 256K token context window as well. This model is actually comparable to top-gun open models like Chimera and DeepSeek for an example. On HLE, it scores about 47.6,'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is a touch below 54 from Chimera, but hey, it\'s better than other models in the same parameter bracket. And it\'s not just HLE either. On Seal Zero, which is a long horizon search benchmark, it scores 56.4 and'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that is actually the overall state of the art. It beats Kimmy K2.6. It beats DeepSeek V4 Pro and it even beats GPT 5.5 on the extra high reasoning setting. A 35B model beating trillion parameter'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scale models at search, that\'s crazy. On GAIA, it gets about 96, which is right behind DeepSeek V4 Pro at 98 and ahead of GPT 5.5 at 87.'

[02:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'On BrowseComp, it gets 75.5, which is the best among the 35B class models, though the frontier models are still clearly ahead there. The instruction following numbers are really good, too. On I bench, it scores 80.6 and on I fail, it scores 94.8.'

[02:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And both of those are overall state-of-the-art results above every frontier model they compared against. This matters a lot for agentic use because an agent that can\'t follow instructions is basically useless, no matter how smart it is.'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In Frontier Science Olympiad, it even outperforms the bigger models. Though, take that with a bit of grain of salt at the very least. It scores 79 there against GPT 5.5 78 and on Frontier Science Research, it scores 40 while GPT'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '5.5 only gets about 27. Now, these numbers are from their own report, so again, grain of salt. But even if they\'re somewhat inflated, the model is clearly doing something right in the science domain, which kind of makes'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sense because Intern Science is literally a science focused lab. This is not a multimodal model, unlike Gemma, which is multimodal. So, this is a bummer for sure. They say that it supports tool use quite well. They say it supports function calling and'

[03:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'external tools, including APIs, code interpreters, search engines, and task environments. It also handles extended conversations and documents while preserving coherence, recall, and multi-step state. Then it also tracks detailed constraints across diverse'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'domains from scientific research prompts to structured tool workflow. The way they trained it is also pretty interesting. It\'s a three-stage process. First, full domain supervised fine-tuning.'

[03:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then they train separate domain-level teacher models. So, one specialist for search, one for code, one for tool use, and so on. Finally, they distill all of those teachers back into one student model. Basically, a bunch of specialists'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compressed into a single generalist. I believe this is going to become the standard recipe for small agentic models because it clearly works. Now, let\'s get straight to the point. So, I won\'t be testing it on the KingBench three prompts. You may ask why.'

